Z||000014||JSocha||07-26-2001||09:29 AM||||The letter "X" can be, represent and stand for anything you want.<P>For example:  The big red "X" one might draw on your X-girlfriend's or X-wife's picture representing your utter hatred in the now love-hate relationship with her that you now hold.  Not to be discriminotory, as it works the opposite way for the ladies to on the male picture.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>It could also be used as a signature as in "X" for the signature of a radical terrorist and of course "Xski" for a Polish terrorist.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>A baker, one who gets paid for loafing, might just use the "X" cut on top of buns or other breads to relieve the cooking pressure that builds up in the dough during baking and make the dough expand more making the bread more full.  Also gives a great place to melt butter when they come out of the oven.<P>The "X" is very universal and there really is not meaning behind it other then the way one may use it in a certain instance.  Especially in Cross stitching (aka x-stitching).  An abreviation; a representation; a mark; a check; a...ahh...anything...<P>Somehow, and I can't believe it, I beat KW to this post with an overly educated response.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (j/k KW!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/jsocha_2.gif"> ||||reg||1
